+# Old origin (`https://dailygrind.example.com`) artifacts
+
+Deploy these files to the **old** origin. The main app in the repo root is now served from
+`https://app.dailygrind.example.com`. Both are same-site (`dailygrind.example.com`), which
+Web App Origin Migration requires.
+
+| File | Purpose |
+| --- | --- |
+| `.well-known/web-app-origin-association` | Old origin authorizes the new app id (`https://app.dailygrind.example.com/`) as its successor. Serve as `application/json`. Make sure the host doesn't hide dot-directories. |
+| `manifest.webmanifest` | Replaces the old manifest. Adds `migrate_to` so installed copies find out about the migration without anyone visiting the new origin. Keep `id` the same as the old manifest's. |
+| `migrate-export.html` | Framed (hidden) by the new origin's `migrate-config.js`. It posts the old origin's localStorage, and only to the new origin. The new origin migration moves only the installation (shortcuts, icons), not storage. |
+| `migration-banner.js` | Add `<script type="module" src="/migration-banner.js"></script>` to old pages. This is the fallback for browsers without origin migration (Firefox, Safari). |
+
+Permissions (notifications and so on) are origin-bound. Users have to grant them again on the new origin.
+
+To make the move mandatory, add `"behavior": "force"` to the `migrate_from` entry in the root
+`manifest.webmanifest`. Users then must either update or remove the app.
