@@ -0,0 +1,76 @@
+# Privacy by Design — The Daily Grind website
+
+This document records how the site applies privacy by design (GDPR Art. 25,
+CPRA, ISO 31700) and is the reference for the automated audit in
+`scripts/privacy-audit.mjs` (`npm run audit:privacy`). Run the audit in CI;
+it fails the build on any regression.
+
+## 1. Principles → implementation
+
+| Principle | Implementation |
+|---|---|
+| **Proactive / privacy as default** | No analytics, ads, tag managers, social widgets, web fonts or CDNs. Optional categories in `js/privacy.js` are default-off. |
+| **Data minimization** | Nothing is written to the device on first visit. Saved choices are 2 booleans + a day-precision timestamp — no IDs. Tracking query params (`utm_*`, `gclid`, `fbclid`, …) are removed from the URL. |
+| **Storage limitation** | Saved choices expire after 180 days and are purged; data for withdrawn categories (e.g. `dg_favorites`) is deleted right away. Logout clears session/local storage, and the server sends `Clear-Site-Data`. |
+| **Respect user signals** | Global Privacy Control and Do Not Track force an opt-out that the UI can't override. `/.well-known/gpc.json` advertises GPC support. |
+| **No leakage to third parties** | All assets (including the hero photo, formerly hot-linked from `images.unsplash.com`) are self-hosted. `Referrer-Policy: same-origin`. Off-site links automatically get `rel="noopener noreferrer"`. |
+| **Security as a privacy control** | Strict CSP with no inline code, HSTS, frame denial, COOP/COEP/CORP isolation, Permissions-Policy that denies sensors and ad-tech APIs (Topics/FLoC). |
+| **Secure state changes** | Logout is a `POST` form, not a GET link, so prefetchers, crawlers and cross-site `<img>` tags can't trigger it. |
+| **Transparency & control** | The footer states the site's practices in one line. "Your Privacy Choices" opens an accessible `<dialog>`; without JS it links to `/privacy-policy#your-choices`. Rejecting is as easy as accepting. |
+
+## 2. Third-party inventory
+
+Reviewed on **2026-11-15**. Policy: a third-party request is added only after
+a documented review (purpose, data shared, legal basis, DPA, retention), with
+an entry here and in `THIRD_PARTY_ALLOWLIST` in the audit script. The CSP will
+block it until the policy is updated too.
+
+| Host | Purpose | Data exposed | Status |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `images.unsplash.com` | Hero photo | Visitor IP, User-Agent, Referer (page URL) | **Removed** — image self-hosted at `assets/img/hero.jpg` (Unsplash License permits this; no EXIF metadata) |
+| *(none)* | | | Current third-party request count: **0** |
+
+### Review checklist for any new third party
+1. Can it be self-hosted or replaced with a first-party option? (Preferred.)
+2. What personal data reaches it (IP, cookies, identifiers, page URLs)?
+3. Legal basis; is consent required? If so, load it only behind
+   `DailyGrindPrivacy.hasConsent('<category>')`.
+4. DPA signed, data location, retention, sub-processors.
+5. Use Subresource Integrity (`integrity` + `crossorigin`) for scripts and styles.
+6. Add the narrowest possible CSP source (exact host and path), and update `_headers`,
+   the `<meta>` CSP, the `npm start` CSP, the audit allowlist and this table.
+
+## 3. Client-side data map
+
+| Key / cookie | Where | Contents | Set when | Retention |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| `dg_privacy_choices` | localStorage | `{v, savedAt(day), choices:{analytics, personalization}}` | User clicks Save/Reject | 180 days, then purged |
+| `dg_favorites` (future) | localStorage | Favorite drink IDs | Only with `personalization` consent | Deleted on withdrawal / logout |
+| Session cookie (server) | Cookie | Opaque session ID | Sign-in | Session; must be `Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax` (or `Strict`), `__Host-` prefix |
+
+The script also purges known tracker cookies and storage keys (`_ga`, `_fbp`,
+`_hj*`, …) left behind by older versions of the site.
+
+## 4. Security headers
+
+The headers live in `_headers` (Netlify/Cloudflare Pages format); copy them to
+any other host or CDN. `npm start` (statikk) sends the CSP plus COOP/COEP
+locally. A `<meta>` CSP in `index.html` is a fallback for hosts that can't set
+headers (`frame-ancestors` only works as a header).
+
+| Header | Value / purpose |
+|---|---|
+| Content-Security-Policy | `default-src 'none'`, self-only scripts/styles/images, `frame-ancestors 'none'`, `form-action 'self'`, `base-uri 'none'` — blocks injected trackers and data exfiltration |
+| Strict-Transport-Security | 2 years, subdomains, preload |
+| Referrer-Policy | `same-origin` — URLs never sent cross-site (`no-referrer` would null the `Origin` header the logout CSRF check relies on) |
+| Permissions-Policy | Denies camera, mic, geolocation, sensors, payment, Topics/FLoC, etc. |
+| COOP / COEP / CORP | Cross-origin isolation; stops other sites from embedding or reading our resources |
+| X-Content-Type-Options / X-Frame-Options | `nosniff` / `DENY` |
+| Cache-Control | `no-store` on account, rewards and order pages; immutable caching only for assets with no personal data |
+| Clear-Site-Data | On `/logout`: `"cache", "cookies", "storage"` |
+| X-Robots-Tag | `noindex` on account pages and internal docs |
+
+## 5. Server-side requirements (outside this repo)
+- `POST /logout`: check the `Origin` header (or a CSRF token), invalidate the session server-side, and respond with `Clear-Site-Data`.
+- Keep access logs to a minimum: truncate IPs (/24 for IPv4, /48 for IPv6), keep for 30 days or less, and don't log query strings on account/order routes.
+- Any future analytics must be first-party, cookieless and aggregate, and run only with `analytics` consent.
